; RENAMED 2026-09-07 (house cull): was `house_secondage_1_level1/HouseSecondage1Level1.tscn`, root node
; `HouseSecondage1Level1`. The House's look pool was cut from 18 variants to 3 — this one plus its two
; `Houses_SecondAge_1_Level*` siblings — and the survivors were renamed to say what the player
; sees rather than where the mesh came from. The style id `WorldRoot._style_id_from_scene_path()`
; derives from THIS filename, so the rename changes the id `house_secondage_1_level_1` -> `house_large`; a save
; holding the old id degrades through `get_style_default()`'s existing stale-id fallback (to
; `model_scenes[0]`), which is the documented behaviour for a renamed scene, not a migration.
; The SOURCE glTF keeps its pack filename (`HousesSecondAge1Level1.gltf`) so the CREDITS.md /
; `quaternius_ultimate_fantasy_rts.tres` `assets_used` provenance line still matches on disk.
; The player-facing label is NOT this filename: `StylePickerPopup._HOUSE_LABELS` maps
; `house_large` -> "House - Large", because `capitalize()` cannot produce the dash.
;
; House look variant — HouseSecondage1Level1 (SecondAge house batch).
; The SecondAge tier of the same pack, artist and palette as the 10 shipped FirstAge
; House variants — imported so the House's `model_scenes` pool can be widened.
; Wiring these into project/data/buildings/house.tres is gameplay-engineer's step, NOT
; done here.
;
; Source: Quaternius, "Ultimate Fantasy RTS" pack (glTF), Houses_SecondAge_1_Level1.gltf — CC0 1.0
; Universal, same pack as every shipped House variant. Pack License.txt re-read on this
; run; no new attribution entry — `assets_used` extended in
; project/attribution/sources/quaternius_ultimate_fantasy_rts.tres.
;
; MATERIAL/TEXTURE AUDIT (measured on the raw, unmodified glTF, same probe method the
; Farm Buildings batch used): albedo_texture is null on all 5 surfaces and NO mesh
; surface carries an ARRAY_COLOR channel at all — color comes entirely from each named
; material's albedo_color (Main 0.6933/0.3491/0.2691, Walls 0.6910/0.6933/0.6520, Stone
; 0.5315/0.5333/0.5015, Wood 0.5333/0.4160/0.2572, Wood_Light 0.6467/0.5303/0.3449, and
; Stone_Light 0.7042/0.7067/0.6645 where present). Wood and Stone are byte-identical to
; the shipped FirstAge variants' two materials, so the SecondAge tier is the same palette,
; not a second one. Nothing to fix; no material_override applied.
;
; TRIANGLES: 2336 across 5 materials (the shipped Houses_FirstAge_1_Level1 is 404
; across 2) — this tier models doors and windows the FirstAge lean-to does not.
;
; SCALE: NONE — 1.0 scale, no transform written at all. Raw AABB measured via manual
; local-transform composition (NOT global_transform — see
; project/data/terrain/cultivated_field.tres's header for why that silently returns
; identity on a freshly-instantiated, not-yet-tree-attached node): footprint 0.889953 x
; 0.864464 tile-units (x, z), height 0.772206. Both footprint axes already sit inside a
; 1x1 tile (WorldGrid.TILE_SIZE = 1.0), so nothing is rescaled and NO judgment call is
; embedded here — this is a measurement, not a proposal, the same posture
; Houses_FirstAge_1_Level1 ships with.
;
; FACING: left as authored (identity transform, no rotation) — unconfirmed by a human eye
; pass, same posture as every other placement judgment call in this project. FLAG FOR
; HUMAN SIGN-OFF.
; RECENTRED 2026-09-07 (house cull), and this is a CORRECTION, not a new judgment call.
; MEASURED: this wrapper's world-composed mesh AABB centred on (-0.094363, _, -0.111007) in the
; wrapper's own space, not on (0, 0) — the mesh is off-centre on its own origin in the source
; glTF. A building is positioned by its TILE CENTRE, so an off-centre mesh hangs over the
; neighbouring tile: at 0.0433 units of overhang this one spilled 0.0394 on X and 0.0433 on Z.
;
; The `Model` node's translation below is exactly the negation of that measured centre
; (+0.094363, 0, +0.111007) — a number read off the mesh, not chosen, so nothing here needs a
; human ruling. X and Z only: Y is left untouched because the model already sits on the ground
; plane and vertical placement is not what a footprint reserves.
;
; WHY THIS SURFACED NOW, since the mesh has been off-centre since it was imported on 2026-08-29:
; `test_building_footprint_alignment.gd` only ever spill-checks the House that actually gets
; placed, which is `model_scenes[0]`. Before the cull that was `house/House.tscn`
; (Houses_FirstAge_1_Level1), which happens to be centred on X exactly and clears the tile on Z;
; the other 17 variants were never placed by any test, so their offsets sat unmeasured. The cull
; promoted this file into the tested slot and the existing assertion caught it immediately. THE
; SCALE FACTOR ABOVE IS UNCHANGED — recentring moves the mesh, it does not resize it.
;
; RESCALED 2026-09-08 — FOOTPRINT-DEEPENING TRIAL (human ruling: "double everything uniformly").
; The eight 1x1 buildables moved to a 2x2 footprint, so the long-horizontal-axis normalisation
; target doubles: 0.869309 -> 1.738618 — the target Barn.tscn's own 2x2 already uses, so this is
; the project's existing 2x2 convention, not a new one. This file's basis scale AND its
; recentring translation are therefore each multiplied by exactly 2 (1 -> 2).
;
; DOUBLING RATHER THAN RE-MEASURING IS DELIBERATE. A uniform factor of 2 preserves every ratio
; already ruled on, so nothing decided earlier is silently reverted — the SCALE and RECENTRED
; notes above still describe their own derivation correctly, at exactly half this file's current
; numbers. The translation doubles for the same reason the scale does: it is the negation of a
; centre measured in post-scale wrapper space, so it scales with the mesh.
;
; THIS IS A TRIAL FOR A LOOK-PASS, NOT A SIGNED-OFF VALUE. The footprint change it serves is
; unratified (spec.md Open Question #18) and the downstream re-tuning it implies is deliberately
; NOT done yet — see the buildable's own .tres header for the outstanding list.
;
; WHY: buildings read too small against a person. `Man.tscn` normalises a villager to exactly
; 1.0 tile height (WorldGrid.TILE_SIZE = 1.0, gdd.md -> "a villager stands ~1 tile tall"), and
; every 1x1 building's scaled mesh stood SHORTER than that — all three House looks (0.772,
; 0.909, 0.706), the Small Barn (0.687) shorter than the 0.734 cow, the Open Barn/stable (0.667)
; shorter than the 0.965 horse. The 0.869309 target was a convention inherited from the FirstAge
; House batch; it was never measured against a person.
;
